From 139cf0849dfa799ced80df71b13d9e1d4ef34f69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 07:10:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] JavaScriptCore: 68k template JIT bit operations on numbers

&, |, ^ keep their int32 code and gain a float path; <<, >>, >>>, ~ and
op_unsigned (after >>>) get fast code of their own, where they had only
the interpreter's handler. An operand that is a double goes to the float
path, which truncates it to int32 from its IEEE bits with integer
shifts (as V8's DoubleToInt32: the significand shifted by the exponent,
low 32 bits kept, negated if negative; NaN and infinities give 0)
instead of calling the C slow path. No FPU work and no FPCR switch.
The truncation is one routine per compiled function, which each operand
calls. Only non-numbers still take the template. A numeric constant operand is its ToInt32 at compile time
(x & 0xffffffff). op_unsigned of a negative int32 makes its double
inline. Loops' register copies take the shifts, ~ and op_unsigned on
int32s; inlined functions may use them. JSC_useM68KBitFastPaths=0 turns
it all off.

Team 3
